00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.610
In our previous
video, we deep dived

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.980
into streamlining your
application deployment.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.960
And now we're onto
the fourth stop,

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.140
which is all about deploying
your microservices application

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.240
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.970
In this video, we're going
to go over understanding

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.190
microservices and
Kubernetes at a high level

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.990
and introduce you to Google
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE,

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.960
and understand a few of
its important features.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.780
We will also be looking at a
sample e-commerce application

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.270
architecture and see
how it will be deployed

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.350
in the demonstration section.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.440
We will finally end it
with a customer success

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.780
story of an actual
Google Cloud customer

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.820
and wrap up with some
actionable next steps.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.880
So without any
further ado, let's get

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.640
right on to exploring the
Google Cloud platform together.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.230
Imagine you and your teammate
have built out a fairly large,

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.100
complex application.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.520
Now let's say you're
done making some changes

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.160
to your part of the
application, and your teammate

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.320
is still working on an
entirely different part

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.240
of the same application.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.700
Wouldn't it be convenient
if you could just test out

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.460
your changes, or even deploy
to the main application

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.700
while your teammate continues
working on his part?

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.440
This sort of a
planning approach can

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.830
be taken if you build out your
architecture with microservices

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.080
as opposed to monoliths.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.380
A monolithic application
is a single unified unit,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.020
whereas microservices
architecture breaks it down

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.920
into a collection of
smaller, independent units.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.560
Containerization is a
method of visualization

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.830
which separates the applications
and services at the operating

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:54.940
level.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.690
And microservices uses
containerization to deliver

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.870
smaller, single-function
modules that work in tandem

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.230
to create more agile and
scalable applications.

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:08.560
And Kubernetes is excellent
for containerization.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.360
Moving on to Kubernetes.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.890
Kubernetes can be
summed up by two things.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.060
One, it's an abstraction
over infrastructure.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.000
What I mean by this is that,
when you install Kubernetes,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.060
it handles the compute,
networking, and storage

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.280
for your user
workloads, so you don't

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.620
have to worry too much about
the underlying environment,

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.260
and you'll be able to
focus on your application.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.890
And secondly, Kubernetes
uses a declarative format

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.930
to orchestrate its containers.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.810
What we mean by that is that
you tell us what you want,

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.210
and not necessarily
how to create it.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.910
Kubernetes runs a
control loop to ensure

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.490
that the current state
always matches the desired

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.700
state, as mentioned, in
your configuration files.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.980
If it doesn't, Kubernetes
is smart enough

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.160
to know what steps to take
to keep them in alignment.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.540
It might all seem
a little complex.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.030
In fact, it is.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:01.060
But that's where Google
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE,

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:02.140
comes in.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.500
GKE is a managed service
for running Kubernetes.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.280
GKE essentially gives you an
abstraction by providing you

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.560
with freedom with
some configurations,

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.020
and also manages
a lot of features

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.580
for your containerised
applications.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.820
So when we say
managed, what we mean

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:23.560
by that is that it manages
your auto-repair, auto-upgrade,

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.010
auto-scaling, auto-provisioning,
and regional or

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:29.020
high-availability code plane.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:32.830
It is also your amazing site
reliability engineering team.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:37.060
We have two modes in GKE,
Standard and Autopilot.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.340
Standard Mode is
where you configure

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.070
the cluster's underlining
infrastructure, giving you

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:44.740
the node configuration
flexibility.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.870
And Autopilot Mode
is a step ahead,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:50.560
where GKE provisions and manages
your cluster's underlying

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.680
infrastructure, including
nodes and node pools,

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.940
giving you an optimized cluster
with hands-off experience.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:00.190
Now let's quickly jump
on to a demonstration

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.860
of configuring different
aspects of a GKE cluster

